  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nOne last short wave under this broad upper level trough regime\nwill provide some chances for precipitation across the area \ntoday with light rain being the primary precipitation type \nacross the lowlands and light snow showers across the mountains.\nForecast soundings indicate the lack of abundant moisture and \nrather a shallow moisture profile which will promote sprinkles \nand flurries across the area. Any accumulations in the mountains\nwill be little to none as much of the activity should be mainly\nflurries. Some light coatings in the highest peaks and ridges \ncould come to fruition although impacts remain very low. Once \nthe upper trough moves further east and cuts off the moisture \nflux from the Great Lakes precipitation will come to an end by \nthis evening.\n\nAs high pressure builds in the cloud coverage should dissipate \nand allow for some valley fog to develop under clear and clam \nconditions for the next several days. The area will become dry \nand commence a slight warming trend to get us back to seasonable\nby midweek. The next chance for any precipitation will be after\nthe new work week.",
